1
00:00:01,685 --> 00:00:13,762
Namo tassa bhagavato hara-hato sanma-sambodhasa Homage to the Buddha, the blessed, noble and fully self-awakened one.

2
00:00:14,983 --> 00:00:25,077
So we're going to look at this Kalama Sutta, which is probably amongst Westerners who get converted, one of these very well-known discourses.

3
00:00:25,428 --> 00:00:27,974
It's probably more widely known than that.

4
00:00:28,675 --> 00:00:36,636
Basically, it's really about the right to ask questions that Buddhism doesn't come at you with, thou shalt believe.

5
00:00:37,088 --> 00:00:49,523
And what happens is, the opening scene, the Buddha arrives at a place called Kesaputta, where he meets these Kalamas, and they're in a state of confusion.

6
00:00:49,823 --> 00:00:54,029
There are some monks and Brahmins, Venerable Sir, who visit Kesaputta.

7
00:00:54,548 --> 00:01:03,179
They expound and explain only their own doctrines, and the doctrines of others they despise, revile, and pull to pieces.

8
00:01:04,156 --> 00:01:06,462
So it's a case of I'm right, nobody else is wrong.

9
00:01:06,802 --> 00:01:14,921
So the Kalamas say, Venerable Sir, there is doubt, there is uncertainty in us concerning them.

10
00:01:15,343 --> 00:01:20,396
Which of these reverend monks and Brahmins spoke the truth and which falsehood?

11
00:01:20,936 --> 00:01:21,337
Okay.

12
00:01:24,052 --> 00:01:35,305
So what we discovered, of course, is that they've got this existential terror of the age, which was what will happen when I die and what my rebirth will be.

13
00:01:36,287 --> 00:01:50,483
And just as an aside, the Greeks and Romans, the ancient world, of course, were sort of afraid of fate, by which they meant what these capricious gods felt like doing.

14
00:01:50,864 --> 00:01:52,325
And they had no power over it.

15
00:01:53,007 --> 00:01:53,787
And

16
00:01:54,004 --> 00:02:03,256
The horror was to end up in Hades, where when you hear descriptions of it, it's a bit like advanced dementia, where you lose your identity.

17
00:02:03,615 --> 00:02:06,478
You're just wandering around aimless in this dark place.

18
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:11,344
And if you get to see the Odyssey, he sort of has a stab at it.

19
00:02:13,106 --> 00:02:18,492
In the medieval ages, of course, it was the sheer existential fear of going to hell.

20
00:02:18,844 --> 00:02:28,282
And that's when the Roman Catholic Church came up with the idea of purgatory, so that only those who committed really bad mortal sins went to hell.

21
00:02:28,342 --> 00:02:35,034
The others could spend their time in a place where they were purified and then go to heaven.

22
00:02:35,776 --> 00:02:40,784
And these days, of course, for people who are scientific materialists,

23
00:02:41,204 --> 00:02:47,393
And it's the abyss, what Nietzsche called the abyss, like death is moving into nothingness.

24
00:02:48,133 --> 00:02:54,484
And when Nietzsche touched upon that, he called it the abyss.

25
00:02:55,544 --> 00:03:00,412
So here we have it, this particular time, it was this whole rebirth thing.

26
00:03:00,693 --> 00:03:07,562
And there were people at the time who were materialists who said, well, no, you know, when your life ends, that's the end of it.

27
00:03:07,763 --> 00:03:09,725
You don't have to worry about what happens afterwards.

28
00:03:11,004 --> 00:03:26,301
So now the Kalama wants to know the truth and he doesn't fall into the error of these other teachers of telling them what he teaches and how everybody else is completely deluded.

29
00:03:26,342 --> 00:03:34,431
So he starts by trying to lead them out of that confusion to give them some guidelines

30
00:03:35,068 --> 00:03:39,778
And he offers this method of freedom to investigate.

31
00:03:40,441 --> 00:03:44,750
So he makes this what's considered a very famous statement.

32
00:03:45,752 --> 00:03:49,280
It is proper for you Kalamas to doubt, to be uncertain.

33
00:03:50,002 --> 00:03:53,449
Uncertainty has arisen in you about what is doubtful.

34
00:03:53,782 --> 00:03:59,471
Come Kalamas, do not go upon what has been acquired by repeated hearing.

35
00:03:59,850 --> 00:04:05,057
And if you think of Trump and the way he keeps repeating his lie until a lot of people believe him.

36
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:15,893
Nor upon tradition, nor upon rumour, nor upon the scriptures, because just something's written down in some holy scripture doesn't mean it's true.

37
00:04:16,497 --> 00:04:23,745
Nor upon surmise, you know, to form an opinion or guess without really having the evidence and then believing it.

38
00:04:25,809 --> 00:04:27,151
Nor upon an axiom.

39
00:04:27,170 --> 00:04:30,636
So something that's universally accepted doesn't mean it's true.

40
00:04:32,757 --> 00:04:38,865
Nor upon a bias towards a notion that has been pondered over.

41
00:04:39,286 --> 00:04:42,471
And so these days we get these information silos.

42
00:04:43,108 --> 00:04:49,841
Not upon another seeming ability, you know, their teaching ability and being drawn into that.

43
00:04:50,261 --> 00:04:52,665
If he can teach so well, he must be talking the truth.

44
00:04:53,646 --> 00:04:58,214
Nor upon the consideration, this monk is our teacher.

45
00:04:59,089 --> 00:05:27,442
kalamas when so so you don't you don't believe it just because your teacher said it but when you yourselves know these things are immoral these things are blameable these things are censured by the wise undertaken and observed these lead to harm and ill when you know that by your own personal experience then he says abandon them so he's rejecting the idea of blind belief

46
00:05:34,173 --> 00:05:41,297
Now, he's not saying that you can decide what you want to believe.

47
00:05:41,564 --> 00:05:50,357
It's not a freedom to believe what you want as if the spiritual life was a sort of smorgasbord and you just choose little bits that you like.

48
00:05:50,898 --> 00:05:53,281
And that often happens to Westerners, doesn't it?

49
00:05:53,562 --> 00:05:59,951
We can see the process of meditation, we like that, but rebirth, well, that's just an idea.

50
00:06:00,312 --> 00:06:02,535
The test, of course, is to do with ethics.

51
00:06:03,115 --> 00:06:07,081
So there's a connection here with rebirth and ethics.

52
00:06:09,879 --> 00:06:14,047
So he doesn't leave them entirely to their own resources.

53
00:06:14,086 --> 00:06:27,949
He says, Kalamas, when you yourselves know these things are bad, these things are blameable, these things are censured by the wise, undertaken and observed, these things lead to harm and ill, then abandon them.

54
00:06:27,970 --> 00:06:33,660
So that's a direct sort of connection with what a person might feel to be unskillful.

55
00:06:34,230 --> 00:06:38,737
And then he begins this sort of Socratic method of questions and answers.

56
00:06:39,079 --> 00:06:40,420
So what do you think, Kalamas?

57
00:06:40,540 --> 00:06:44,387
Does greed manifest in someone for their benefit or for their harm?

58
00:06:44,887 --> 00:06:46,471
For their harm, Venerable Sir.

59
00:06:46,870 --> 00:06:58,209
Kalamas, being given to greed and being overwhelmed and vanquished mentally by greed, such a person takes life, steals, commits adultery and tells lies.

60
00:06:58,529 --> 00:07:01,233
They also prompt others to do likewise.

61
00:07:01,213 --> 00:07:04,637
Will that not eventually be harmful and damaging to them?

62
00:07:05,197 --> 00:07:06,218
Yes, Venerable Sir.

63
00:07:06,899 --> 00:07:10,463
And of course, he repeats that with hatred and delusion.

64
00:07:12,064 --> 00:07:16,228
And then he says, so he's continuing this process of questioning them.

65
00:07:16,870 --> 00:07:22,696
And in a sense, he's teaching them as I think Socrates had wanted to do.

66
00:07:23,255 --> 00:07:24,637
So then he continues this.

67
00:07:24,697 --> 00:07:30,663
And I think this is the way that we understand Socrates to have wanted people to...

68
00:07:31,083 --> 00:07:36,091
to find a way in which they can question themselves.

69
00:07:36,892 --> 00:07:38,175
So what do you think, Kalamas?

70
00:07:38,656 --> 00:07:40,358
Are these things good or bad?

71
00:07:40,699 --> 00:07:41,762
Bad, Venerable Sir.

72
00:07:42,322 --> 00:07:44,646
Blameable or not blameable?

73
00:07:45,908 --> 00:07:47,132
Blameable, Venerable Sir.

74
00:07:47,232 --> 00:07:48,995
Censured and praised by the wise?

75
00:07:49,475 --> 00:07:50,658
Censured, Venerable Sir.

76
00:07:51,038 --> 00:07:54,745
Undertaken and observed, do these things lead to harm or ill?

77
00:07:55,264 --> 00:07:57,288
Or how does it strike you?

78
00:07:57,728 --> 00:07:58,690
So what do you think?

79
00:07:58,790 --> 00:08:05,341
And they say, well, undertaken and observed, these things lead to harm and ill. That's very clear to us.

80
00:08:08,065 --> 00:08:09,548
And then, of course, he does the opposite.

81
00:08:10,250 --> 00:08:11,351
What do you think, Kalamas?

82
00:08:11,372 --> 00:08:15,298
Does absence of greed manifest in someone for their benefit and harm?

83
00:08:15,798 --> 00:08:18,723
For their benefit, venerable sir, and so on.

84
00:08:21,252 --> 00:08:33,610
So the question, of course, is how do we know that something is genuinely true, wise or beneficial?

85
00:08:34,371 --> 00:08:46,450
So remember that we are prone to confirmation bias, you know, just listening and talking to people who agree with us and always self-affirming our own beliefs.

86
00:08:47,408 --> 00:08:55,659
So the Buddha talks about the three ingredients that go into freezing us into a sort of wrong place.

87
00:08:55,980 --> 00:09:03,029
So that's the initial wrong understanding, the wrong view, our emotional commitment to it.

88
00:09:03,530 --> 00:09:08,958
And of course, most entrapping is a sense of identity.

89
00:09:09,379 --> 00:09:11,863
So as soon as you say, I am a...

90
00:09:12,399 --> 00:09:18,048
And whatever that finishes with, I am a Buddhist, then you're trapped, as it were, into that.

91
00:09:18,109 --> 00:09:25,801
Now, of course, some entrapments can be skillful, but he's particularly interested in entrapments that are not.

92
00:09:27,903 --> 00:09:32,910
So in the Kalama Sutta, he's giving them permission to think for themselves.

93
00:09:33,351 --> 00:09:34,594
Now, having done that,

94
00:09:35,047 --> 00:09:43,511
He now gives them the exercise in which they can establish, we can say, a good mind, a good heart.

95
00:09:44,193 --> 00:09:46,440
And that, of course, is the practice of metta.

96
00:09:47,062 --> 00:10:05,846
the disciples of the noble ones kalamas who in this way is devoid of coveting devoid of ill-will undiluted clearly comprehending and mindful dwells pervading one quarter with the thought of love likewise the second likewise the third likewise the fourth

97
00:10:05,826 --> 00:10:20,822
So above, below and across, he dwells, encompassing all sentient beings everywhere in the entire cosmos with the abundant, exalted, boundless thought of love that is free of hatred and malice.

98
00:10:21,042 --> 00:10:23,985
And he does the same for compassion, joy and equanimity.

99
00:10:24,625 --> 00:10:34,634
And here, of course, he's giving them an exercise which, in terms of rebirth, would, you know, angle them towards the happy realms.

100
00:10:36,842 --> 00:10:54,846
So now he offers these four consolations or assurances, having understood what the central concern of the Kalamas were, which was about rebirth and its connection to your karma.

101
00:10:56,467 --> 00:11:03,677
Supposing there is a hereafter and there is a fruit result of deeds done well and ill.

102
00:11:04,113 --> 00:11:13,201
then it is possible that at the dissolution of the body after death, I shall arise in the heavenly world, which is possessed of the state of bliss.

103
00:11:14,082 --> 00:11:17,407
So that's your first consolation if you take his arguments.

104
00:11:18,086 --> 00:11:25,414
But suppose, he says, there is no hereafter, and there is no fruit, no result of deeds done well or ill.

105
00:11:25,875 --> 00:11:33,342
Yet, in this world, here and now, free from hatred, free from malice, safe and sound,

106
00:11:33,981 --> 00:11:35,705
I keep myself.

107
00:11:35,725 --> 00:11:38,549
So this is the second consolation.

108
00:11:38,570 --> 00:11:41,033
So whether there is or not, it's better to live this way.

109
00:11:42,375 --> 00:11:45,902
Suppose evil results befall an evildoer.

110
00:11:46,842 --> 00:11:49,648
I, however, don't think of doing harm to anyone.

111
00:11:50,188 --> 00:11:55,136
Then how can ill results affect me, who has not harmed anyone?

112
00:11:56,078 --> 00:11:58,722
You will find this as your third consolation.

113
00:11:59,446 --> 00:12:24,019
assurance after his teachings and the final one is supposing evil results do not befall an evildoer then i see myself purified in any case so the teaching here the basic line is that the inner happiness of moral integrity surpasses anything which is momentary superficial pleasure

114
00:12:25,837 --> 00:12:29,522
And this, of course, really sort of settles the Kalamas.

115
00:12:29,562 --> 00:12:37,631
Now, he's given them a way of investigating what they've been told.

116
00:12:38,472 --> 00:12:46,802
And he's given them this leeway to decide for themselves whether there is karmic results and whether there is a rebirth or not.

117
00:12:47,442 --> 00:12:53,009
And this seems to have inspired the Kalamas so much that they took the Buddha as their teacher.

118
00:12:55,370 --> 00:13:06,100
So here we have this lovely discourse, which, you know, he enters into this confusion, into these people who are confused.

119
00:13:06,961 --> 00:13:14,269
His intention is to help them out of the confusion, not to convince them of he's right and everybody else is wrong.

120
00:13:14,730 --> 00:13:22,719
So he seems to have that ability to see where you are and to take you from that place to a deeper understanding.

121
00:13:22,839 --> 00:13:24,921
I mean, he's a great teacher.

122
00:13:25,542 --> 00:13:51,634
and he offers them this freedom to inquire he then gives them ethics as the first criterion of truth and recognizing bias and distorted judgment so he's addressing their problems around karma and rebirth and he does this especially through these four assurances and of course he's given them this matter this brahma laharas which will lift their heart

123
00:13:52,221 --> 00:14:09,048
And when he sees that they now see him as perhaps their teacher, you can presume that either later in the day, because perhaps he arrived in the morning and now he's been offered some food and he takes a rest.

124
00:14:09,451 --> 00:14:10,451
The siesta.

125
00:14:10,652 --> 00:14:11,153
Yeah.

126
00:14:11,173 --> 00:14:12,173
And then he comes back.

127
00:14:12,475 --> 00:14:21,144
And then, of course, seeing that these people have been, shall we say, affected by his teaching, he probably hits them with the Four Noble Truths.

128
00:14:21,164 --> 00:14:26,250
And if he doesn't think they're ready, then he'll come back and visit them at a later date.

129
00:14:27,451 --> 00:14:35,981
So here we have a lovely story which tells us a lot about the Buddha and just his remarkable ability to teach people.

130
00:14:38,695 --> 00:14:39,517
Very good.

131
00:14:39,537 --> 00:14:59,995
I can only hope my words have been of some assistance, that they have not caused confusion, and that by your devotion to investigating the truth and making sure that you practice, you will arrive at that wonderful place, free of suffering, nibbana, sooner rather than later.

